𝗗𝗼 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝘁𝗵𝗶𝗻𝗸 𝘆𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝗝𝗲𝗻𝗸𝗶𝗻𝘀 𝗣𝗶𝗽𝗲𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲 𝗶𝘀 𝗶𝗺𝗺𝘂𝗻𝗲 𝘁𝗼 𝗛𝗮𝗰𝗸𝗲𝗿’𝘀 𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗮𝗰𝗸? 

Here are some of tips to secure your Jenkins Pipeline from Hacker’s attack and enhance security of your workspace 👇🏻

✨ 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗲 𝗝𝗲𝗻𝗸𝗶𝗻𝘀 𝗜𝘁𝘀𝗲𝗹𝗳:
Access Control - Enable Auth using Jenkins over Database or integrate with OAuth, SSO or LDAP.
Use RBAC (Role-based Access Control) to restrict access based on roles (admin/devs/testers).
Use HTTPS over Jenkins to encrypt traffic.
Install Plugins from trusted source, remove unused plugins.

✨  𝗦𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗲 𝗝𝗲𝗻𝗸𝗶𝗻𝘀 𝗔𝗴𝗲𝗻𝘁𝘀 (𝗦𝗹𝗮𝘃𝗲𝘀):
Run them with 𝗹𝗶𝗺𝗶𝘁𝗲𝗱 𝗽𝗿𝗶𝘃𝗶𝗹𝗲𝗴𝗲𝘀
Use Agent-to-controller communication using SSH with Authentication
Isolate Build Env using containers (docker-in-docker) or K8s pods.

✨  𝗣𝗿𝗼𝘁𝗲𝗰𝘁 𝘀𝗲𝗰𝗿𝗲𝘁𝘀 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗖𝗿𝗲𝗱𝘀
Use Jenkins Credentials to store: SSH keys, AWS keys, Github token, dockerhub creds, etc.
Use withCredentials() block in pipeline rather than hardcoding secrets.

✨ 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗿𝗶𝗰𝘁 𝗝𝗼𝗯 𝗘𝘅𝗲𝗰𝘂𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻
Set 𝗯𝘂𝗶𝗹𝗱 𝗽𝗲𝗿𝗺𝗶𝘀𝘀𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀 so only authorized users can trigger builds
Use 𝗮𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝘃𝗮𝗹 𝗴𝗮𝘁𝗲𝘀 for sensitive jobs like production deployments.
Enable 𝗮𝘂𝗱𝗶𝘁 𝘁𝗿𝗮𝗶𝗹 𝗽𝗹𝘂𝗴𝗶𝗻𝘀 to track who triggered what and when.

✨ 𝗘𝗻𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗖𝗼𝗱𝗲 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗗𝗲𝗽𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗲𝗻𝗰𝘆 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸
Integrate tools like SonarQube, Trivy, Owasp Dependency check, Synk to enhance Code quality check and dependency check

✨ 𝗟𝗼𝗴𝗴𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗠𝗼𝗻𝗶𝘁𝗼𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴
Enable Audit Trail plugin or Logstash plugin for logging
Monitor Jenkins Logs for suspicious login attempts, failed build or permissions error.

🌟 𝗕𝗼𝗻𝘂𝘀 𝗧𝗶𝗽:
Define Pipeline and Infrastructure using Jenkinsfile, Terraform, Helm, etc.
use Git-Ops tools like ArgoCd to have git as single source of truth.
